WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.719
Hank: Hello, and welcome to the very first
episode of "Dear Hank and John"

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:07.120
John: Or, as I like to think of it, "Dear
John and Hank".

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.210
We are John and Hank Green, and this podcast
is where we...

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.349
What, what do we do here Hank?

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:13.349
Hank: Uh...

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:16.940
This is the podcast where we answer your questions;
we are going to provide some dubious advice,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.700
that we are not qualified to give; and also
give you all the week's news from Mars, the

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.830
planet, and also AFC Wimbledon, the 18th tier
English soccer club.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.670
But first, John, how about a very short poem
for today.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:31.910
John: Fourth tier for the record.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.809
But, okay, we are going to start with a Langston
Hughes poem for today:

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:37.190
"Folks, I'm telling you,
birthing is hard

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.530
and dying is mean-
so get yourself

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.320
a little loving
in between."

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:48.390
John: That brings us nicely, Hank, to today's
first question, which is from Lindy, she writes:

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.570
"Dear John and Hank.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.481
Hey, so there's this girl I really really
like, maybe even love, I don't know, this

00:00:53.481 --> 00:00:54.481
is confusing.

00:00:54.481 --> 00:01:00.729
I have felt like this for 6 years, the problem
is that I am also a girl, and she is straight.

00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:03.750
How do I stop liking them as more than a friend?"

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.500
Hank: John, I just want to say, thank you
so much for starting us off with the hardest

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.520
question ever.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.520
John: Yep.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.970
Hank: How do you stop loving a person, John?

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.140
John: Yep.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.140
Umm...

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.330
Not my area of expertise.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:22.470
My area of expertise is continuing to love
someone even when it's a bad idea.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.470
Umm...

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.250
I thought this was more of a question for
Hank.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.310
This seems like something that Hank would
be good at.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.350
Hank: Uhh...

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.500
Right, well, just... just, if you say so.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.190
Bury yourself in other things.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:43.390
Just get really obsessed with bowling, and
just think, like, take all the energy that

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:49.040
you are focusing on this lovely person, and
focus it on getting...

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.480
being a really good bowler.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.020
Or, uhh..., or you know, probably something
more useful that bowling would be a good idea.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.220
John: Hank, do you know that that is literally
what I did my senior year of college?

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.200
Are you aware of that or was that-- did you
just coming up with bowling randomly?

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.200
Hank: No!

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.060
No, I looked over and I was like "I have to
find something" and I saw my bowling pin that

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:14.780
I won for getting-- I got a 199 that day and,
uh..., and so I won this bowling pin as a

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:15.780
trophy.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.739
And that's what I saw so that's what I-- that's
what I hit.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:22.640
John: I, uh, I was in a long term relationship
that ended and I was still very much in love

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.510
with this, uh, young woman that I was in a
relationship with.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.239
And one of the things that I took up in the
wake of the relationship was bowling.

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:32.299
And I became an extremely passionate bowler.

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:35.721
I bowled every single day by myself at the
bowling alley in Mt.

00:02:35.721 --> 00:02:37.760
Vernon, Ohio and I just loved it.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.590
It was a wonderful release for me.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.090
It was repetitious in a way that soothed my
brain.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.829
I'm a huge fan of bowling and when you call
bowling useless, as I believe you just did,

00:02:48.829 --> 00:02:49.829
it hurts me.

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:50.829
It hurts me deep down.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:56.430
Hank: I just-- I mean, if you're gonna spend
500 hours getting over someone, which is at

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.069
least as long as it takes, you might wanna
focus on, like, maybe something like playing

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:08.019
guitar or accordion or maybe learning, uh...,
theoretical physics.

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:09.069
I don't know.

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:10.069
Bowling comes in handy.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.400
I definitely go bowling and when I go bowling
and someone's really good at bowling I'm like

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.400
"Yeah!

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.540
You're really good at bowling!" and I think
that about them.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.849
So, it's not like... completely useless.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:22.250
Uh... it's as useless as being good at any
other sport.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.689
Except that it's better because bowling is
something that everyone can enjoy together.

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:31.779
Uh, even if you're not bowling I feel like
you enjoy bowling just as much as people who

00:03:31.779 --> 00:03:32.779
are.

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:35.470
If I'm going to the bowling alley and, like,
my shoulder hurts, which it does sometimes,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.470
and I don't bowl.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.470
I still have a good time!

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.389
Because bowling is really more about sitting
around.

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:44.870
John: So Lindy, I think, um..., I think we've
arrived upon a solution for you.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:51.849
Um..., it's not easy, uh..., to change your
feelings about someone or to wait out your

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:57.430
feelings about someone or to sublimate them,
but the key is bowling!

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.430
Your welcome!

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.689
God, we're so good at advice!

00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:00.689
Hank: Yeah!

00:04:00.689 --> 00:04:01.999
John: Um, this one is from Lucas.

00:04:01.999 --> 00:04:06.969
"Dear John and Hank, What is your favorite
thing to look at in the night sky?"

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:07.969
Hank: Well.

00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:11.829
Yeah, I, um, there's a lot to choose from,
I think I'm gonna cop, like, go with a cop

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:16.699
out answer here and say the Milky Way, 'cause
if you can see it, which you can't always

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:20.280
in a lot of the country, but here in Montana,
we often can.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.620
If you can see the Milky Way, it is the most
amazing, awe-inspiring, like, tear-inducing,

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.870
put-you-in-your-placing thing that you can
see.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:32.620
Um, it's a really beautiful thing, and it's
a really beautiful thing that you can see,

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.960
it's a really beautiful thing and it's a really
beautiful experience to like, really look

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.200
at the galaxy and be like, there it is, and
I can only see it in that direction, which

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.110
means that we're sittin' out here on the edge
of it, and it's a big beautiful thing.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:50.810
I think that that is by far the most sort
of beautiful thing you can see in the night

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.080
sky with your naked eye.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.620
Of course, it's--I'm saying it's a cop out
because it is not a thing, it is many billion

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.620
things.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.759
John: For me, it's the middle star in Orion's
belt.

00:05:00.759 --> 00:05:03.740
What I like to think of as Orion's belt buckle.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.810
That's my favorite thing to look at.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:08.550
That was a very fancy answer, Hank, but I
like Orion's belt buckle.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.669
It just, it reminds me of where I am in the
world.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:19.030
Hank: That star is called Alnilam, and it
is a, a super g--a blue supergiant, which

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:27.460
is a big, a big star, and it is only 1,340
light years away, John, and yes, I did just

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:28.620
look that up on Wikipedia.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.940
John: Quick question about blue giant stars.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.639
Are they about to die?

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:37.139
Like, what is, what are the chances that I'm
going to stop enjoying Orion's belt buckle

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:40.319
because it turns into a supernova?

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:44.650
Hank: I think that you're safe for your lifetime.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:45.650
John: Okay.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:50.100
What about like, young people watching this
podcast, or listening to it?

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:57.020
Hank: I don't know offhand about the average
age of a blue supergiant, but in general,

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.699
the lifetime of a human is not consequential.

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:03.439
John: Ah, the lifetime of a human is not consequential.

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:06.039
You really, one really does come here to get
the good news.

00:06:06.039 --> 00:06:07.289
John: Do you have a question?

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:08.630
Hank: I do.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.539
"Dear Hank and John", this is from Claire.

00:06:12.539 --> 00:06:18.229
"I graduated from high school three days ago,
and next year I'm going to a very prestigious

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:19.229
university."

00:06:19.229 --> 00:06:22.790
Well, thanks for letting us know how prestigious
your university is, Claire.

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.620
"This is good," she says.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:30.409
"I'm very happy that the black magic of college
admissions somehow spun in my favor, but this

00:06:30.409 --> 00:06:34.930
school, it is expensive and has lots of amazing
resources across the board from engineering

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.280
to the arts and back.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:40.280
I just want to milk my 32 courses for all
their worth, so I'm curious, what do you guys

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.639
think, what should I study to be the biggest
help to the world?

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:47.259
Please assume for the sake of argument that
I'm equally good at all academic disciplines,

00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:50.319
which, while untrue, is my rhetorical stance."

00:06:50.319 --> 00:06:52.080
John: That is a fascinating question.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.409
I think Claire is in for big things, no matter
what she does, given the quality of her question

00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:00.389
asking.

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:01.840
I'm interested to know what you think, Hank.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.830
Hank: I'm super into the idea of having a
really diverse base of knowledge.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:12.830
So if you got 32 classes, you know, you're
gonna have to focus on something because they

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:21.490
require you to major in things in college,
but do your best to have a broad, a broad

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:27.659
experience and like, as long as everything
that you're taking is in some way challenging,

00:07:27.659 --> 00:07:31.610
so it like--that's what you want, you want
to be challenged, and you want to not hate

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:35.219
your life, so that's what you should try and
do with college.

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:37.080
John: And everything else.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:38.420
Hank: Yeah, and your entire life.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:43.230
John: I am a huge Roger Ebert fan, Hank, the
great film critic who was also something of

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:48.449
a public intellectual, like, he wasn't, he
studied film and, you know, wrote film reviews

00:07:48.449 --> 00:07:54.069
for most of his career, but he also studied
a lot of other things, and had a lot of other

00:07:54.069 --> 00:07:58.440
fields that he found interesting, and I thought
that idea, like, I feel like that idea of

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:04.030
the public intellectual has sort of fallen
away in this era of specialization, and there

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:08.070
are great things about specialization, but
I completely agree with you, um, when you

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:14.210
specialize in unexpected ways, when you have--when
you can make unexpected connections between

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:18.560
worlds that people think of as separate, that
really makes you stand out, so I think that's

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.439
the, that's the best of what higher education
has to offer now, and I totally agree that

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:24.970
Claire should embrace that.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:30.349
John: Hank, we have another question, this
is from Ava, "Dear John and Hank, will you

00:08:30.349 --> 00:08:34.250
come to our wedding in British Columbia on
July 11th?"

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.430
Ava, let me just answer before Hank does,
because Hank lives very close to British Columbia,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:41.680
not at all inconvenient to him, he has no
children, I can't see a reason why he wouldn't

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:47.820
go, unless he's just a jerk, but I can't go,
because I will be supporting the movie adaptation

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.500
of my book Paper Towns, and I have previous
commitments.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:53.790
I'm so sorry, Ava, but I'm sure that Hank
is gonna say yes.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:57.459
Hank: I am doing colonoscopy prep doing that
day, so.

00:08:57.459 --> 00:08:59.810
John: Wow, that's actually a fantastic excuse.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:06.450
Hank: Yeah, I gotta, gotta drink a bunch of
awful snot that makes me turn into a poop

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.450
volcano.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:12.230
John: That is one high-quality excuse for
not attending a wedding.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:13.230
Congratulations.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.829
And congratulations to you as well, Ava.

00:09:15.829 --> 00:09:17.050
We will send a gift.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:18.510
Hank: Will we?

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:19.780
I think this is the gift.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.070
John: No, no, no, we'll send a gift, we'll
get them something.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.630
She didn't even tell us where she was registered.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.370
I don't feel like I got a formal invitation,
y'know?

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:31.790
She just e-mailed hankandjohn@gmail.com, I
didn't--there was nothing letter-pressed,

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:36.300
I don't like to criticize peoples' wedding
invitation strategies, because certainly I

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:40.560
think the wedding industry is a bit bloated,
but I mean, y'know, you could have sent something

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:41.560
in the mail, Ava.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.420
Hank: Okay, here's another question, this
one is from Sam.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:48.670
Sam says, "Dear Hank and John, do you guys
have faith that humans will eventually colonize

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.670
the galaxy?"

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:50.680
John: NO.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.320
I can answer that in one word.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.320
No.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.320
I do not think that we will ever colonize
the galaxy.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.250
The galaxy is very--all the other stars are
so far away.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.680
Hank: I--I was really interested to hear your
answer to this question, John, because I didn't--I

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.120
honestly didn't know which way you were gonna
go.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.240
I think we already have.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.470
John: What?

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:14.040
Hank: Just a very small part of the galaxy,
we have colonized.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.490
John: Do you mean Earth and Mars?

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:16.949
Hank: No, I mean Earth.

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:18.930
Just Earth.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:23.440
John: We have done an amazing job of colonizing
Earth, I mean, we--

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.829
Hank: We have colonized the crap out Earth.

00:10:26.829 --> 00:10:27.829
John--own this planet.

00:10:27.829 --> 00:10:28.829
Hank: Yeah.

00:10:28.829 --> 00:10:34.060
John: There is hardly a square inch of this
planet that humans have not either lived in

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:35.529
or peed in.

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:40.230
Hank: We definitely--humans have peed on every
single part of the planet, definitely.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:45.010
John: Yeah, no, there is--you can't find us
everywhere on this planet, but you can find

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:46.010
our waste everywhere.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:47.010
Hank: Yeah.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:52.320
I would actually be interested to see a map
of where humans haven't peed.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:57.769
I bet there's some places, I mean, there's
certainly some places in Antarctica and maybe

00:10:57.769 --> 00:10:59.270
the Sahara were humans haven't peed.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:03.200
John: Oh, there's large swaths of the ocean
where we haven't peed, but I would argue that

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.130
our pee is filtered out into the rest of the
ocean, I mean, if you pee--

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:10.980
Hank: Oh, certainly, once you pee in the ocean,
once, you've peed in the entire ocean.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:16.480
John: Yeah, so if you've peed in the Atlantic,
you've, by the law of transitive ocean peeing,

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.920
you've also peed in the Pacific, because it's
all connected.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:26.100
It's really just one body of water that we
have named various oceans, so you've peed

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.519
in the one human--the one Earth ocean.

00:11:28.519 --> 00:11:29.519
Hank: That's true.

00:11:29.519 --> 00:11:30.519
That's true.

00:11:30.519 --> 00:11:32.700
John: Really, if you've peed in any body of
water that's con--if you've peed in a river,

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.040
you've peed in the ocean.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:35.329
This is getting pretty meta, Hank--

00:11:35.329 --> 00:11:36.329
Hank: Yeah.

00:11:36.329 --> 00:11:42.079
John:--but when I peed in the White River,
I technically peed in the, in the Indian Ocean.

00:11:42.079 --> 00:11:43.550
Hank: Here's a question for you.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:47.610
If you've peed in the White River, did you
pee into a cloud?

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:53.649
Because eventually that water that was in
your pee, is--some of it's gonna end up in

00:11:53.649 --> 00:11:54.649
clouds.

00:11:54.649 --> 00:12:00.339
John: Not just in clouds, Hank, but also it's
gonna end up in rain that's gonna end up falling

00:12:00.339 --> 00:12:01.930
on Antarctica.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:02.930
Does it rain there?

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:03.930
I don't know.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:09.430
Hank: It doesn't--(laughs) The question is,
did you pee on your own head?

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:13.959
John: I'm more concerned with whether I have
successfully peed on all seven continents,

00:12:13.959 --> 00:12:18.390
and you refuse to answer that, because you
also don't know if it rains in Antarctica.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:24.490
Sam, if we can zoom out of your question a
little bit, if you're referring to us colonizing

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:34.940
non-solar system things, there's no way in
10,000 years we could even get to another

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:38.120
planetary--it just--it's not possible, unfortunately.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.190
It would be awesome.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:41.860
I love Star Trek, it's just not possible.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:46.420
Hank: My serious answer to Sam is that in
10,000 years, no.

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.320
In 100,000 years, I don't know.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:56.459
In a million years, if humans are still around,
we may have found ways to inhabit other star

00:12:56.459 --> 00:13:03.670
systems, it is--it would be a very slow process,
unless some kind of faster than light travel

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:09.270
was developed in which case, more power to
us, but I feel like if faster than light travel

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:12.670
was possible, we would have seen aliens by
now.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:18.290
John: Let me just back up and respond to a
different part of that, which is that your

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:24.029
proposal that human beings might exist in
a million years, which is ludicrous.

00:13:24.029 --> 00:13:29.660
Of all the things, of all the ridiculous things
involved in colonizing other stars in the

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:35.540
galaxy, the part where we still have humans
in a million years is the most ridiculous.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.920
Hank: Are you saying million or billion?

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.170
John: Million with an M.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:42.190
Hank: I completely disagree with you, it is
entirely possible that there will still be

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.190
humans in a million years.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:44.190
John: I mean--

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.190
Hank: Why would you, why would you--

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:48.600
John: I will bet you a hundred dollars right
now that there will be no humans in a million

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.600
years.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.440
Hank: I take the bet!

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.779
John: Alright.

00:13:53.779 --> 00:13:54.779
I can't lose.

00:13:54.779 --> 00:13:55.779
I'm not gonna lose.

00:13:55.779 --> 00:13:59.459
There is--we, we are lucky if we have 5,000
years.

00:13:59.459 --> 00:14:01.920
Our species is insane.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:08.200
It's insa--the greed, the consumption, the,
the, the mutually assured self-destruction,

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:11.980
oh, we are in the second half of being people,
for sure.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.310
For sure!

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:15.864
Hank: I--well, even if we are in the second
half of being people...

00:14:15.864 --> 00:14:21.399
John: I know, we might, we might have up to
100,000 years, at the very, the very best

00:14:21.399 --> 00:14:23.019
that could happen, 100,000 years.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:27.420
Hank: There's a--there's a lot, there's a
lot of potential future, but there are also

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.779
many, many species that have been around for
longer than a million years.

00:14:30.779 --> 00:14:34.110
They may not be any--in any way like us--

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.110
John: Right, yeah.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.110
Hank: --but--

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:41.500
John: I don't know, that's like saying 'this
rock is really old, so I bet I'll live forever,

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:42.500
too.'

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:43.990
Hank: Alright.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:47.740
I--I think I--I think that the future is unknowable.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:51.220
John: Why don't--you--why don't you ask the
next question?

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:56.480
Hank: "Dear Hank and John, What if a guy says
I'm too smart for him?"

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:57.660
John: This question came from Rebecca.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.180
Here is my advice, Rebecca.

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:05.710
So I want you to go to a mirror, find a mirror
somewhere, and then I always find it helpful

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:08.420
to practice my faces in the mirror, Hank,
do you know what I mean?

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.170
Like, I just try to practice--

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:11.170
Hank: Yeah.

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.970
John: --so that I know what people are seeing
when I make a face.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.829
That if I'm trying to communicate an emotion
with my face, I want them to--I wanna know

00:15:17.829 --> 00:15:21.329
what they're seeing, you know, like what my
happy face looks like, et cetera?

00:15:21.329 --> 00:15:22.329
Hank: Mhmm.

00:15:22.329 --> 00:15:26.200
John: So, Rebecca, I want you to go to a mirror,
and I want you to put on a face of horror.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:30.060
Like, uh, like just horrified.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:35.560
And then I want you to like, slightly transfigure
that face to add in intense disgust.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.460
And then I want you to hold on to that face,
figure out what it looks like, and the next

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:43.790
time someone tells you that, that, that you
are too smart for them, I want you to make

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:49.579
that face, and then I want you to say, "No,
no, no, no, no, no, you are no longer under

00:15:49.579 --> 00:15:51.000
consideration."

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:55.070
And then back away and move on with your life.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:56.070
Take up bowling.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:57.070
Hank: Take up bowling.

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:03.410
I--yeah, I think the answer to that question
or the response to 'you're too smart for me'

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.510
is, 'yeah, I think you're right.'

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:10.279
John: (Laughs) "I'm glad that we're on the
same page about this.

00:16:10.279 --> 00:16:14.370
I didn't know that I was too smart for you
until just now but thank you for confirming

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:15.370
it."

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:19.019
Hank: (Laughs) "Thanks, thanks for letting
me know."

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:24.139
John: Hank, we only have, uh, we only have
time in this first podcast for one more question.

00:16:24.139 --> 00:16:28.850
Um, it comes from Egide, I hope I said your
name right Egide.

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:30.569
"Dear John and Hank.

00:16:30.569 --> 00:16:33.220
I really like what you guys do on YouTube.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:37.250
I was just wondering, who does your parents
think is the better son?"

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:38.759
Hank: Oh wow.

00:16:38.759 --> 00:16:39.759
Um...

00:16:39.759 --> 00:16:40.769
John: That's easy.

00:16:40.769 --> 00:16:42.279
Hank: Is it?

00:16:42.279 --> 00:16:46.000
John: Let's just, let's just answer on three,
OK?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:51.710
We'll answer together, that way your opinion
won't shape mine or anything.

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:53.040
We'll just answer on three.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.040
Hank: OK.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.220
John: 1, 2, 3.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:56.220
Hank.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:57.220
Hank: Dave.

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:58.220
John: No!

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.220
You are cheating!

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:02.079
That's not, that's total cheating!

00:17:02.079 --> 00:17:06.059
I stayed true to the rules of the game that
I invented and you cheated.

00:17:06.059 --> 00:17:07.059
Typical!

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:09.760
Hank: I just think that our parents really
like Dave.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.020
John: They do like Dave.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:16.339
John: Alright Hank, it's time for the news
from Mars, which is a small planet on the

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:21.760
edge of the Milky Way galaxy, and the news
from AFC Wimbledon, which is the most important

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:23.819
soccer team in the history of the world.

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:26.329
Hank: That's what we do at the end of the
podcast, John.

00:17:26.329 --> 00:17:27.329
Why do we do it?

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:31.680
John: Uh, well, because you love Mars, I love
AFC Wimbledon, and people love to learn about

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:32.929
Mars and AFC Wimbledon.

00:17:32.929 --> 00:17:37.870
Hank: This week NASA is testing the Low-Density
Supersonic Deceleration System which is also

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.700
called the LDSD.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:44.220
John: Because God forbid NASA makes something
without having an appropriate acronym.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:46.850
Hank: Well, you gotta write, it's a lot of
words.

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:49.299
You've gotta have a fast way to write it down.

00:17:49.299 --> 00:17:54.770
So the LDSD's job is to deliver larger payloads
than we can currently deliver to the surface

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:55.830
off Mars.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:01.919
There's this problem that you have when you're
travelling interplanetarily that you are going

00:18:01.919 --> 00:18:06.630
very fast when you arrive at your destination
and you have to slow down and it is difficult

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:07.640
to slow down.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:10.830
So one of the things that they do to slow
down is they just hit the atmosphere and let

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:16.310
the atmosphere slow the craft down and then
eventually open parachutes and the parachutes

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:21.050
will further slow the craft down and then,
like at the last moment, you fire some rockets

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:22.050
maybe.

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:25.470
The problem that Mars has is that there isn't
a ton of air in the atmosphere so parachutes

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:29.780
won't open higher in the atmosphere so you
can't slow down as fast as you would like

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:32.120
especially if your craft is super heavy.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:39.780
The job of the LDSD is to, uh, it basically
inflates to make the craft bigger.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:45.530
It's like a bunch of balloons, like airbags
on the side of the craft that catch the atmosphere,

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:49.890
increase its surface area so it can slow down
faster before deploying the parachutes.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.860
And that increases the payloads we can deliver
to Mars from, like, one and a half tons to

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:57.510
up to three tons which is twice as much.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:58.510
Very exciting.

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:02.440
John: So I just want to confirm, Hank, that
the most interesting thing that happened on

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:04.750
Mars this week happened on Earth.

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:08.250
Hank: Well that is almost always gonna be
the case.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:14.680
It's gonna be more the news in Mars and regarding
Mars.

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:19.270
John: When Hank and I initially came up with
the idea for this podcast we were like "Oh,

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:24.140
and we'll do the news from Mars and AFC Wimbledon"
and then Hank literally said to me "But is

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:27.429
there news from AFC Wimbledon every week?"

00:19:27.429 --> 00:19:31.809
And I'm like "Is there news from Mars every
week?"

00:19:31.809 --> 00:19:38.570
Hank: I, like, I just don't understand your
proposition that somehow AFC Wimbledon and

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:42.030
Mars are on the same plane of interest.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.120
John: No, listen.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:46.960
To be clear, I am not proposing that AFC Wimbledon
and Mars are on the same plane.

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:51.360
I am proposing that AFC Wimbledon is in fact
far more important than Mars.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:53.330
Hank: Yeah, I have a really hard time with
this.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:20:00.100
I mean, if you look at the amount of, like,
attention and money spent on one of those

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:03.230
two things, much more attention and money
is spent on Mars.

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:04.900
Like, this is a true fact.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:08.130
John: Mars is a cold, dead rock.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:14.140
Much like any of the gajillions of cold dead
rocks scattered throughout the universe.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:17.190
Not only that, but you can find, you can find
cold dead rocks right here on earth.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.320
You don't have to go to space to get one.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:24.169
However, you do have to go to South London
to get AFC Wimbledon.

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:28.559
So, can we get, instead of arguing about whether
Mars is more interesting than AFC Wimbledon,

00:20:28.559 --> 00:20:31.880
which is a settled question because AFC Wimbledon
is more interesting, can we get to the news

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:33.169
from AFC Wimbledon this week?

00:20:33.169 --> 00:20:34.500
Hank: That is acceptable.

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:40.890
John: Okay Hank, so AFC Wimbledon, the fourth-tier
English soccer team that plays in South London,

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:44.310
has three on-the-uniform spots for sponsorship.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:45.310
Right.

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:48.630
So there's the front of the shirt, there's
the back of the shirt, and then there's the

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.659
back of the shorts.

00:20:50.659 --> 00:20:51.659
You know what I'm talking about?

00:20:51.659 --> 00:20:52.659
Hank: Yeah yeah yeah.

00:20:52.659 --> 00:20:55.049
John: So you've got like a corporate name
on each of those places.

00:20:55.049 --> 00:21:02.070
So Hank, can you guess what savvy marketing-oriented
corporation will be advertising itself on

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:07.830
the lower left buttock of next season's AFC
Wimbledon uniform?

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:09.340
Can you guess?

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:11.580
Hank: Is it, uh, DFTBA Records?

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:13.740
John: It's us!

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:14.740
That's right!

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:15.740
It is us.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:22.289
Their shorts will again say DFTBA Nerdfighteria
right there in that wonderful liminal space

00:21:22.289 --> 00:21:24.710
between left thigh and left buttock.

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:31.740
I am so excited to be able to announce this
week that the 2015-2016 AFC Wimbledon uniform

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:37.539
will again remind its players and opponents
who are running behind its players not to

00:21:37.539 --> 00:21:39.130
forget to be awesome.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.770
I'm very excited.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:45.510
The season is over for AFC Wimbledon, but
of course the news AFC Wimbledon-wise continues

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:50.940
year-round so we'll have plenty to discuss
in this exciting off-season, as AFC Wimbledon

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:55.690
attempts to strengthen their squad to really
push up, to maybe next year make it to the

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:57.840
third tier of English football.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:03.900
Hank: And as I'm going to attempt to feign
interest in your weird interests, how are

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.669
they doing these days, John?

00:22:05.669 --> 00:22:09.890
John: Well Hank, as I just told you, the season
has ended, so they aren't currently playing.

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:11.710
Hank: But how did they do?

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:12.710
How did they do?

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:13.710
Did they do okay?

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.570
John: What do you mean "how did they do?"

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:19.730
Do you never look to see how their doing?

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:20.890
What do you do all day?

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:25.360
Like I don't understand what you look at on
the internet if you aren't looking at least

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:29.120
occasionally at soccer standings in fourth-tier
English football.

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:32.090
Like, what is it on the internet that you
look at instead?

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.050
Hank: ...Mars stuff.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:35.350
John: Aaalright.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:41.650
They finished 15th in League 2 this year,
so-called because it's the fourth league of

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:42.650
English football.

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:46.000
It goes the Premier League, then the Championship,
then League 1, then League 2, and then you

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.610
aren't in the league anymore, you're not a
professional team.

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:50.090
So you want to stay at least in League 2.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:55.000
So they finished 15th in League 2, comfortably
mid-table, but next season we'll be looking

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:01.409
to really push toward the top half of that
table and maybe even into the top few spots

00:23:01.409 --> 00:23:02.850
that get up to League 1.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:04.980
Hank: This sounds complicated, John.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:10.539
John: Not as complicated as building a thing
to go on Mars the size of a minivan.

00:23:10.539 --> 00:23:13.730
Hank: Yeah, and also not as cool.

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:15.210
(John laughs)

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.429
John: So what did we learn today, Hank?

00:23:17.429 --> 00:23:23.390
Hank: We learned that love is hard, and that
we're not going to go to Ava's wedding.

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:24.710
John: Yup.

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:29.700
We also learned that we're probably not going
to go to colonize other stars in the galaxy,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:39.510
and we learned that while love is hard, and
it's hard to move on from love, there is always

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:44.929
what Emily Dickinson so beautifully called
"hope, that thing with feathers."

00:23:44.929 --> 00:23:47.170
Which today comes in the form of bowling.

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:49.730
Hank: And the human lifespan is insignificant.

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:53.929
John: Thanks for listening to Dear Hank &amp; John,
or as I like to think of it, Dear John &amp; Hank.

00:23:53.929 --> 00:24:00.380
You can email us your questions and problems
and anything else that you want to email us,

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:02.480
at hankandjohn@gmail.com.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:05.980
Obviously Hank registered that email address.

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:10.700
But thanks again for listening and as we say
in our hometown of nerdfighteria...

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:11.340
Hank: Don't forget to be awesome.

